---
import architecture from '../assets/server-architecture.png';
import design from '../assets/server-design.png';
import Plate from './Plate.astro';

const parts = [
  { name: 'Aluminum enclosure', role: 'Compact, durable housing for the compute board.' },
  { name: 'Thermal spreader', role: 'Distributes heat from the compute board for quiet, stable operation.' },
  { name: 'Raspberry Pi compute board', role: 'Single-board computer for onboard data processing and control.' },
  { name: 'Local storage', role: 'Solid-state storage for data, models and event buffering.' },
  { name: 'Wireless antenna', role: 'Sits behind an RF window for local wireless links, such as Wi-Fi or Bluetooth LE.' },
  { name: 'Power and network I/O', role: 'Recessed connectors for power and wired networking.' },
];

const pipeline = [
  {
    name: 'Quality check',
    text: 'Each window of readings is checked for skin contact, missing data and motion. An unreliable window is never scored.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Local ML inference',
    text: 'A model compares reliable windows with the astronaut’s own pattern. This runs on the server, so it needs no link to Earth.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Event buffer',
    text: 'When a deviation persists, an event is written to local storage. Events are kept in order and wait there while the link is down.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Earth sync',
    text: 'The server hands queued events to the spacecraft’s communication system. They reach the ground team when that link allows, with its delay.',
  },
];
---
<section class="section" id="server">
  <p class="eyebrow">02 / Onboard server</p>
  <h2 class="section-title">Local analysis continues when the Earth link is unavailable.</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">
    The server is where readings become answers. It sits on the spacecraft, next to the
    astronaut, not on the ground.
  </p>
  <p>
    A Raspberry Pi single-board computer in a sealed aluminum enclosure receives the
    wristband's readings over a local wireless link. It runs the analysis, keeps a bounded
    history and stores events. It does not talk to Earth by itself: Earth sync uses
    spacecraft communications, whenever the mission has a link.
  </p>

  <Plate
    image={architecture}
    variant="render"
    title="Onboard server: compute architecture"
    alt="Exploded view of the onboard server: enclosure lid, thermal spreader, Raspberry Pi board, storage module and base with connectors, above a four-step flow from quality check to Earth sync. The components and steps are described in the lists that follow."
  >
    Exploded view. Proposed component layout.
  </Plate>

  <ol class="parts">
    {parts.map((part, i) => (
      <li>
        <p class="part-index">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</p>
        <h3>{part.name}</h3>
        <p>{part.role}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">From a reading to the ground team</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
    {pipeline.map((step) => (
      <li>
        <h4>{step.name}</h4>
        <p>{step.text}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <Plate
    image={design}
    variant="blueprint"
    title="Onboard server: design study"
    alt="Design study of the onboard server with top, front, rear and isometric views, and a section showing the internal layout."
  >
    Design study: top, front, rear and isometric views, with a section through the internal
    layout. Called out: aluminum enclosure, thermal spreader, Raspberry Pi board, storage,
    antenna behind the RF window, power and network ports.
  </Plate>
</section>
